{
  "$comment": "Read-only inspection commands, merged by hand into ~/.claude/settings.json (allow and ask; the installer does not apply this file). An allow rule covers its command with every flag, so each flag that would let one of these reads run a program or write a file has an ask rule here, and ask wins over allow: git diff/log/show/blame --output=FILE, git fetch --upload-pack, kubectl/helm --kubeconfig (a kubeconfig's exec credential plugin runs a program), kubectl --cache-dir/--profile-output, helm --post-renderer/--output-dir/--repository-cache/--repository-config/--registry-config/--dependency-update, terraform plan -out/-generate-config-out. Left out: terraform fmt without -check (rewrites files) and terraform providers lock/mirror (write files). Edge cases in on purpose: git fetch talks to the network (it writes only .git/), terraform plan runs the configuration's providers and external data sources against the configured backend (it does not apply), terraform validate/show/state show start the provider plugins terraform init put in .terraform/, and shell redirection (> file) can still write a file after any rule. Build and test runners live in permissions-trusted-repo.json.",
  "permissions": {
    "allow": [
      "Bash(git status)",
      "Bash(git status *)",
      "Bash(git diff *)",
      "Bash(git log *)",
